IMPORT prelude

-- Young Offenders Act 1994 (Western Australia) -- who is a young person (s 3, s 4); what police may do instead of charging: no
-- action, a caution, or a referral to a juvenile justice team (ss 22A, 22B, 22, 23, 25, 27, 29, 41); when a team may proceed and
-- dispose of a matter (s 3 "independent young person", ss 30, 31, 32(2)); what completing the team's terms does (s 33); the
-- Department's record of the matter (s 14); and, for the court path the lives do not take, when a conviction is recorded (s 55).
--
-- Edition: compilation from legislation.wa.gov.au (mrdoc 49140, retrieved 7 September 2026), as deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_49140.txt. The deposit names no compilation number. Its compilation table ends with the
-- Community Protection (Offender Reporting) Amendment Act 2024 Pt 3 Div 2 Subdiv 4 (in force 31 December 2025); the last reprint
-- listed is Reprint 7 as at 1 June 2018. The uncommenced provisions table lists, among others, the Young Offenders and Prisons
-- Legislation Amendment Act 2024 Pt 3 (to be proclaimed; not shown, not encoded).
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Requirement au/wa:REQ-0010 (priority 1), raised 4 times by the Western Australian cradle-to-grave life of Jake
-- Harding (born 2006-08-30): k022 (2021-11-20, aged 15, $89 of goods taken from a shop; "What may police do with a 15-year-old
-- instead of charging him, and which court if they charge?"), k023 (2022-02-14, the juvenile justice team; "What does a completed
-- action plan do to the matter?"), k036 (2026-11-20; "Does a juvenile justice team outcome leave a record that can be spent?") and
-- k082 (2047-03-04; "Must he disclose the 2021 matter?"). The ledger lists s 7, ss 22-23, ss 24-30 and s 30. SCOPED to those
-- questions, with the definitions, conditions and exceptions needed to answer them. The scenario cites "ss 29-30 (effect of
-- completing the action plan)" and "s 30 (no conviction recorded)"; in this compilation s 30 is the role of the responsible adult
-- and the effect of completing the team's terms is s 33. Both are encoded.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: which court hears a charge (s 3 defines "court" as "the Children's Court or other court dealing with a young person
-- for an offence"; the Children's Court of Western Australia Act 1988 s 19 is a separate subject); s 7 and s 24 principles beyond
-- the comments below (they guide discretion and decide nothing alone); the contents of Schedules 1 and 2 (whether an offence is a
-- Schedule 1 or Schedule 2 offence is an INPUT); Part 3 (administration), Part 4 (custody and bail), Part 6 beyond s 41, Part 7
-- beyond s 55, and everything after. Whether an infringement notice can be given for an offence depends on other written laws and
-- regulations not retrieved; it is an INPUT. The Spent Convictions Act 1988, which k036 and k082 also cite, is a separate subject.
-- Whether the number of previous offences makes a caution alone inappropriate (s 23) is the officer's judgment; it is an INPUT.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules conventions. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. NOT for public use. See NOTES.md.

§ `Young Offenders Act 1994 (WA) -- diversion from court`

§§ `helpers`

-- Dates are given as year, month and day; the age is in completed years on the given day.
GIVEN `birth year` IS A NUMBER, `birth month` IS A NUMBER, `birth day` IS A NUMBER, `on year` IS A NUMBER, `on month` IS A NUMBER, `on day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`age in completed years` MEANS
    IF `on month` GREATER THAN `birth month` OR (`on month` EQUALS `birth month` AND `on day` AT LEAST `birth day`)
    THEN `on year` MINUS `birth year`
    ELSE `on year` MINUS `birth year` MINUS 1

§§ `3, 4 -- young person`

-- s 3: "young person means -- (a) a person who has not reached the age of 18 years; or (b) a person to whom this Act applies because
-- of section 4".
-- s 4: "If a person commits or allegedly commits an offence before reaching the age of 18 years, this Act applies to the person as a
-- young person for purposes connected with that offence or any order that was made in dealing with the person for that offence."
-- So a man of 40 is still a young person for the purposes of an offence alleged at 15.
GIVEN `age at the alleged offence` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Act applies to him as a young person for that offence` IF
    `age at the alleged offence` LESS THAN 18

-- s 3: "independent young person means a young person who -- (a) has reached the age of 17 years; and (b) has sufficient maturity
-- to live independently without the guidance or control of a responsible adult, and is doing so".
GIVEN `age now` IS A NUMBER, `mature enough to live independently and doing so` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `he is an independent young person` IF
        `age now` AT LEAST 17
    AND `mature enough to live independently and doing so`

§§ `the matter`

-- One alleged offence by a young person, as the police find it.
DECLARE Matter HAS
    `age at the alleged offence` IS A NUMBER
    `there is enough evidence to charge him` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a Schedule 1 or Schedule 2 offence` IS A BOOLEAN
    `an offence under Weapons Act 1999 s 6(1), 6A(1) or (2) or 6B(1) or (2)` IS A BOOLEAN
    `previous offences charged or dealt with under Part 5 make a caution alone inappropriate` IS A BOOLEAN
    `an infringement notice can be given for the offence` IS A BOOLEAN
    `circumstances make an infringement notice inappropriate` IS A BOOLEAN
    `he accepts responsibility for the act` IS A BOOLEAN
    `he agrees to a team rather than a court` IS A BOOLEAN
    `every potential participant agrees to a team` IS A BOOLEAN
    `he has previously offended against the law` IS A BOOLEAN
    `he has been cautioned before` IS A BOOLEAN

§§ `22A, 22B, 22, 23 -- cautions`

-- s 22A: the purpose is "diverting a child who commits an offence from the courts' criminal justice system by allowing a police
-- officer to administer a caution to the young person instead of starting a proceeding for the offence."
-- s 22B: before starting a proceeding a police officer "must first consider whether in all the circumstances it would be more
-- appropriate -- (a) to take no action; or (b) administer a caution to the young person."
-- s 22(1): where a member of the Police Force "could charge a young person", the member "may, having regard to the circumstances,
-- caution the person instead of laying a charge." (2) Orally or in writing. (3) Not if the offence is "(a) a Schedule 1 offence or a
-- Schedule 2 offence; or (b) an offence under the Weapons Act 1999 section 6(1), 6A(1) or (2) or 6B(1) or (2)."
-- (4) An admission made at or about the time of the caution "is not admissible in civil or other proceedings".
GIVEN `the matter` IS A Matter
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `police may caution him instead of charging` IF
        `the Act applies to him as a young person for that offence` (`the matter`'s `age at the alleged offence`)
    AND `the matter`'s `there is enough evidence to charge him`
    AND (NOT `the matter`'s `a Schedule 1 or Schedule 2 offence`)
    AND (NOT `the matter`'s `an offence under Weapons Act 1999 s 6(1), 6A(1) or (2) or 6B(1) or (2)`)

-- s 23(1): police are "to use the power to caution in preference to laying a charge against a young person unless because of the
-- number of previous offences with which the person has been charged or for which the person has been dealt with under this Part
-- it would be inappropriate only to give a caution." (2) The seriousness of the offence and of previous offences counts.
-- The preference is over LAYING A CHARGE. A referral to a team (s 27) is not laying a charge, so s 23 does not rank a caution above a
-- referral. And the test looks back to offences charged or dealt with under Part 5 -- not to a second offence the same night.
GIVEN `the matter` IS A Matter
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a caution is to be preferred to a charge` IF
        `police may caution him instead of charging` `the matter`
    AND (NOT `the matter`'s `previous offences charged or dealt with under Part 5 make a caution alone inappropriate`)

§§ `24, 25, 27, 29 -- referral to a juvenile justice team`

-- s 24: in this Division, particular regard to principles including that treatment "should be in proportion to the seriousness of
-- the offence" and that it "is to be made clear to a young person ... what it is that the person is required to do."
-- s 27: "Where there is sufficient evidence to justify charging a young person with the commission of an offence, a person who could
-- lay the charge may, having regard to the circumstances, refer the matter for consideration by a juvenile justice team instead of
-- laying a charge."
-- s 25(1): not if the offence is "a Schedule 1 offence or a Schedule 2 offence". (4) Only if the alleged offender "accepts
-- responsibility for the act or omission constituting the offence, and agrees to having the matter dealt with by a juvenile justice
-- team rather than by a court"; that acceptance is not an admission or a plea of guilty if the matter goes back. (5) Not "if any one
-- potential participant in the particular proceedings of the team does not agree".
-- The Weapons Act exclusion in s 22(3)(b) is NOT repeated in s 25: a weapons offence that cannot be cautioned can be referred.
GIVEN `the matter` IS A Matter
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `police may refer the matter to a juvenile justice team` IF
        `the Act applies to him as a young person for that offence` (`the matter`'s `age at the alleged offence`)
    AND `the matter`'s `there is enough evidence to charge him`
    AND (NOT `the matter`'s `a Schedule 1 or Schedule 2 offence`)
    AND `the matter`'s `he accepts responsibility for the act`
    AND `the matter`'s `he agrees to a team rather than a court`
    AND `the matter`'s `every potential participant agrees to a team`

-- s 25(2): "If an offence is one for which an infringement notice can be given, the giving of an infringement notice for the offence
-- is to be preferred to referring the matter to a juvenile justice team unless there are circumstances that make the giving of an
-- infringement notice inappropriate."
GIVEN `the matter` IS A Matter
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `an infringement notice is to be preferred to a referral` IF
        `the matter`'s `an infringement notice can be given for the offence`
    AND (NOT `the matter`'s `circumstances make an infringement notice inappropriate`)

-- s 29(1): "The discretion given by section 27 or 28 is to be exercised in favour of referring the matter to a juvenile justice team
-- if the young person has not previously offended against the law." (2) Not "previously offended" merely because he has been
-- cautioned under s 22, given an infringement notice, accepted responsibility under s 25(4), or agreed to or complied with a team's
-- terms. The input `he has previously offended against the law` therefore excludes those; a past caution is recorded separately
-- and does not count.
GIVEN `the matter` IS A Matter
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the discretion is to be exercised in favour of referral` IF
        `police may refer the matter to a juvenile justice team` `the matter`
    AND (NOT `the matter`'s `he has previously offended against the law`)

§§ `41 -- what police are to consider`

-- s 41: a police officer "is to consider whether, in the circumstances, it is appropriate -- (a) to give the person a caution for the
-- offence in accordance with section 22; (b) under section 27, to refer the matter for consideration by a juvenile justice team;
-- (c) to charge the person without taking the person into custody; (d) to apprehend, charge and -- (i) subject to the Bail Act 1982
-- and section 19(2), detain the person in custody pending the person's appearance in court; or (ii) release the person pending the
-- consideration of the matter by a juvenile justice team." s 22B adds "(a) to take no action".
-- Whether detention is lawful under the Bail Act 1982 and s 19(2) is not encoded.
DECLARE PoliceOption IS ONE OF
    `take no action`
    `give a caution`
    `refer the matter to a juvenile justice team`
    `charge him without taking him into custody`
    `apprehend, charge and detain him pending court`

GIVEN `the option` IS A PoliceOption, `the matter` IS A Matter
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the option is open to police` IF
    CONSIDER `the option`
    WHEN `take no action` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `give a caution` THEN `police may caution him instead of charging` `the matter`
    WHEN `refer the matter to a juvenile justice team` THEN `police may refer the matter to a juvenile justice team` `the matter`
    OTHERWISE `the matter`'s `there is enough evidence to charge him`

§§ `30, 31, 32(2) -- when the team may proceed and dispose of the matter`

-- s 30(1): the team "is to give to a responsible adult notice ... and it can only proceed if a responsible adult is present and has
-- indicated agreement with the proposal and a willingness to participate". (2) If a responsible adult is present, the matter "can
-- only be disposed of by the team in a way to which the responsible adult agrees." (3) If the team "considers the young person to be
-- an independent young person, it may dispense with the requirements of subsection (1)." (4)-(5) If no responsible adult can be found
-- or notice would be inappropriate, the team may appoint a parent, relative, employer or other suitable adult to act in place of one.
-- s 31(2): if a victim is present, the matter can only be disposed of by the team if the victim agrees to its being disposed of by
-- the team and to the way.
-- s 32(2): if the young person is absent without good reason, or a party withdraws agreement or will not agree to the terms, the
-- team is to send the matter back.
DECLARE TeamHearing HAS
    `a responsible adult was notified, is present and agrees to take part` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a substitute adult was appointed under s 30(4)` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the team considers him an independent young person` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a responsible adult present agrees to the way of disposal` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a victim is present` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the victim agrees to the team and to the way of disposal` IS A BOOLEAN
    `he is present or has a good reason` IS A BOOLEAN
    `he agrees to the terms` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the hearing` IS A TeamHearing
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the team may proceed` IF
        `the hearing`'s `a responsible adult was notified, is present and agrees to take part`
    OR  `the hearing`'s `a substitute adult was appointed under s 30(4)`
    OR  `the hearing`'s `the team considers him an independent young person`

GIVEN `the hearing` IS A TeamHearing
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the team may dispose of the matter` IF
        `the team may proceed` `the hearing`
    AND (    (NOT `the hearing`'s `a responsible adult was notified, is present and agrees to take part`)
         OR  `the hearing`'s `a responsible adult present agrees to the way of disposal`)
    AND (    (NOT `the hearing`'s `a victim is present`)
         OR  `the hearing`'s `the victim agrees to the team and to the way of disposal`)
    AND `the hearing`'s `he is present or has a good reason`
    AND `the hearing`'s `he agrees to the terms`

§§ `33 -- the effect of completing the team's terms`

-- s 33(1): "Referral of a matter to a juvenile justice team instead of laying a charge does not prevent a charge from being
-- subsequently laid". (2) "If a young person has complied with the terms specified by a juvenile justice team dealing with the person
-- for an offence, a court hearing a charge of the offence, upon being satisfied that the person has complied with the terms, must
-- dismiss the charge without determining it." (3) If he has not complied, a court later dealing with him, "upon being satisfied that
-- the person has taken steps towards complying with the terms, is to have regard to that, and the extent to which the person did
-- comply".
-- So the Act does not say a completed plan ends the matter or bars a charge; it says a later charge must be dismissed undetermined.
DECLARE CourtOutcome IS ONE OF
    `the charge must be dismissed without being determined`
    `the court deals with him, having regard to the steps he took`
    `the court deals with him`

GIVEN `complied with the team's terms` IS A BOOLEAN, `took steps towards complying` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A CourtOutcome
`what a court must do with a later charge` MEANS
    IF `complied with the team's terms`
    THEN `the charge must be dismissed without being determined`
    ELSE IF `took steps towards complying`
         THEN `the court deals with him, having regard to the steps he took`
         ELSE `the court deals with him`

-- s 29(2)(c): a young person who "has agreed to comply or has complied with the terms specified by a juvenile justice team" is not
-- for that reason "to be taken to have previously offended against the law". A completed team matter therefore does not cost him
-- the s 29(1) preference for referral next time.

§§ `14 -- the Department's record`

-- s 14(1): "The chief executive officer is to keep records of every young person who is detained or who is dealt with under this Act
-- for an offence." (2) They include "(b) details of any detention or any way in which the young person has been dealt with for an
-- offence" and "(d) details as to the way in which -- (i) terms specified by a juvenile justice team in dealing with an alleged
-- offender are being, or have been, complied with". (3) "the regulations may provide for the destruction or disposal of records on
-- the basis of their age or such other criteria as are prescribed" (not retrieved).
-- s 15(3)(b)(i)-(ii): the CEO is to tell a person the CEO considers to have "a proper interest in the matter" how an alleged
-- offender "is being, or has been, dealt with by a juvenile justice team" and how the terms were complied with.
-- A team matter is "dealt with under this Act", so a record is kept, though no court finding or conviction exists.
GIVEN `dealt with under this Act for an offence` IS A BOOLEAN, `detained` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the chief executive officer must keep a record of him` IF
    `dealt with under this Act for an offence` OR `detained`

§§ `55 -- when a court records a conviction`

-- s 55(1): if the court "(a) finds a young person guilty of a Schedule 1 offence or a Schedule 2 offence; or (b) finds a young person
-- guilty of any offence and imposes a custodial sentence, the court is required to record a conviction unless it is prevented from
-- doing so by subsection (5) or it is satisfied that there are exceptional reasons for not doing so."
-- (2): other offences without a custodial sentence -- "the court is not to record a conviction unless it is satisfied that there are
-- exceptional reasons for doing so." (5): if under s 66 or 67 the court refrains from imposing any punishment, "the court is not to
-- record a conviction."
-- INFERENCE: s 55 operates only on a finding of guilt; a matter that never reaches a finding (a caution, a team, a charge dismissed
-- under s 33(2)) has no conviction to record.
GIVEN `found guilty` IS A BOOLEAN, `a Schedule 1 or Schedule 2 offence` IS A BOOLEAN, `a custodial sentence` IS A BOOLEAN, `the court refrains from any punishment under s 66 or 67` IS A BOOLEAN, `exceptional reasons` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a conviction is recorded` IF
        `found guilty`
    AND (NOT `the court refrains from any punishment under s 66 or 67`)
    AND (    ((`a Schedule 1 or Schedule 2 offence` OR `a custodial sentence`) AND (NOT `exceptional reasons`))
         OR  ((NOT `a Schedule 1 or Schedule 2 offence`) AND (NOT `a custodial sentence`) AND `exceptional reasons`))

-- s 55(4): "Although a conviction is not recorded, the offender is deemed to have been convicted for the purpose of the making, under
-- this Act or any other written law, of any order that may be, or is required to be, made upon convicting a person of such an offence
-- or for the purpose of the operation of any provision of a road law ... relating to the cancellation of, or disqualification from
-- holding or obtaining, a driver's licence ... or for the purpose of an appeal under the Criminal Appeals Act 2004 (but not for any
-- other purpose)."
DECLARE Purpose IS ONE OF
    `making an order that follows a conviction`
    `a road law cancelling or disqualifying a driver's licence`
    `an appeal under the Criminal Appeals Act 2004`
    `answering an employer's question about convictions`

GIVEN `the purpose` IS A Purpose
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `he is deemed convicted for the purpose though no conviction was recorded` IF
    CONSIDER `the purpose`
    WHEN `answering an employer's question about convictions` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE
